          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "A migraine is a recurrent neurological disorder primarily characterized by frequent headaches. The typical migraine headache is usually one-sided, pulsating, and moderate to severe in intensity. It can worsen with routine physical activity and is often accompanied by symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, and sensitivity to light and sound. In most individuals, migraines are episodic, but in some cases, they can become chronic, defined as having headaches on fifteen or more days each month, with at least eight of those headaches exhibiting migraine features.\n\nIn contrast, ordinary headaches, particularly tension-type headaches, usually present as a dull, aching sensation across the forehead or back of the head and neck. They generally do not come with the severe symptoms associated with migraines.\n\nIf you have more specific questions about migraines or headaches, please consult with a healthcare professional for tailored advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
